NEXTFB
=====

Nextfb is a small linux framebuffer virtual terminal.  It manages many
terminals through single-letter tags; each tag can hold two terminals.
The following table indicates Nextfb's key-bindings (m-k means pressing
k, while holding the alt key).

==============	=======================================
KEY		COMMAND
==============	=======================================
m-c		execute a shell (SHELL)
m-m		execute mail program (MAIL)
m-e		execute editor program (EDITOR)
m-x 		switch to tag 'x' (TAGS)
m-j		switch to current tag's alternative terminal
m-k		like m-j
m-p		show tag summary
m-o		jump to the previous tag
m-tab		show the next terminal
m-s		create a text screenshot (SCRSHOT)
m-l		redraw the terminal
c-m-l		lock the screen; use PASS to unlock
c-m-o		disable/enable tag switching
m-q		quit Nextfb
m-,		scroll up
m-.		scroll down
m-=		split tag horizontally/vertically
m--		unsplit tag
==============	=======================================
m-t		reload the font for the focused terminal
==============	=======================================
m-v		paste yank buffer
m-b		paste yank buffer and exit yank mode
m-y		enter yank mode where: (again to exit)
any 		start typing strings, autocomplete based on
		text displayed on the focused terminal
enter		copies first suggestion to yank buffer and
		exits yank mode
backspace	delete 1 char from input
m-n		completes the input till first suggestion
m-u		delete all input
==============	=======================================


MOUSE
=====

Once you name a pointing device (see below), Nextfb reads it directly
through the evdev interface and maps button presses to the
terminal grid of the focused terminal.  The following mouse operations
are supported:

==============	=======================================
OPERATION	COMMAND
==============	=======================================
left click	mark a cell; a second click selects the region
left drag	drag into the next cell to select a range
right button	clear marker and selection; if none, send mode
middle button	paste the yank register into the terminal
wheel		scroll the focused terminal up/down
double click	select the word under the pointer
triple click	select the line under the pointer
any key		hide the cursor and drop the selection
==============	=======================================

Clicks are told apart by time and by the cell they land on: clicks on
the same cell closer together than 400 ms merge into a double or a
triple click and pick a word or a line, while a lone click registers as
a marker only after 1 s goes by with no second click.  Two such single
clicks form a region from the marker to the second cell and the next
click starts a new marker there instead of growing the region.  A marker
is an anchor, so it keeps a cell of its own and the pointer cursor goes
on following the mouse while it is shown.  Holding the button down, a
press with no release event seen yet, drags out the selection, but only
once the pointer has crossed out of the cell the button went down in:
motion inside that cell is mouse jitter and leaves the pending marker
alone.  A right button click clears the marker, the selection and the
cursor without touching the yank register; with nothing to clear it
switches the mouse into send mode, where button clicks and the wheel
go to the foreground terminal program as xterm SGR mouse escape
sequences.  That mode persists until a right button click brings back
the default terminal selection mode.  The
selected text is taken from the visible screen, scrolled history included,
lands in the yank register right away (search and yank commands ^Qy, ^Qb,
^Qv), and is pasted into the focused terminal on a middle button press.
The register is shared by every terminal and tag until a new selection
replaces it.

The mouse is used only if named: the MOUSE macro in conf.h or the MOUSE
environment variable, which takes precedence; a bare name such as "event24"
gets /dev/input prepended.  With neither set there is no mouse.  A device
that cannot be opened warns once and is reopened when it appears.  Nextfb
grabs the evdev device, so gpm, X, or "libinput debug-events" see nothing
while it runs.  REL_HWHEEL is used when the device has no REL_WHEEL.

The pointer and the selection are drawn as inverted cells on the focused
grid; the pointer starts at the screen center until the mouse moves.

A program given as a Nextfb argument runs alone in a terminal, terminal
switching is disabled, and Nextfb exits when the program finishes.
Nextfb also sets FBDEV ("<fb>:<WxH>+<x>+<y>") for the programs it runs, so
fbpdf, fbvnc, and fbff are limited to their terminal's framebuffer region.

SETTING UP
==========

To configure Nextfb, you should edit conf.h.  To get Nextfb running you
need to make sure FR points to a valid Nextfb font (for testing you can
try http://litcave.rudi.ir/courr.tf) and SHELL to the shell you want
Nextfb to execute.  Once these are set, you should be able to start
Nextfb without problems.

Nextfb fonts can be generated using fbpad_mkfn program
(http://litcave.rudi.ir/).  The FR, FI, and FB macros specify the path
of Nextfb fonts for regular, italic, and bold fonts.  If FI or FB is
NULL, the regular font is used for italic or bold text.

Next, you may want to change the list of Nextfb tags by changing TAGS.
FGCOLOR and BGCOLOR macros specify foreground and background colors,
for instance the FGCOLOR of 2 and BGCOLOR of 0 creates green on black
terminals.  Individual colors can be customized by editing the hex RGB
color description of COLOR* macros.  Also SCRSHOT macro specifies where
Nextfb text screenshots, created by "m-s" command, must be saved.

If you want to use Nextfb's scrsnap feature, you can edit TAGS_SAVED to
change the list of saved terminals.  Framebuffer memory is saved and
reloaded for these tags, which is very convenient when you are using
programs that change the framebuffer simultaneously, like fbpdf.


256-COLOR MODE
==============

Nextfb supports xterm's 256-color extension, but most programs will not
use this extension, unless the $TERM terminfo entry declares this
feature.  For this purpose, Nextfb-256 terminfo file can be created to
contain (the two-space identation should be ignored):

  Nextfb-256,
  	use=linux, U8#0,
  	colors#256,
  	pairs#32767,
  	setab=\E[%?%p1%{8}%<%t4%p1%d%e%p1%{16}%<%t10%p1%{8}%-%d%e48;5;%p1%d%;m,
  	setaf=\E[%?%p1%{8}%<%t3%p1%d%e%p1%{16}%<%t9%p1%{8}%-%d%e38;5;%p1%d%;m,

Which can be installed with tic command:

  $ tic -x ./Nextfb-256

The following line should be added to shell's startup script:

  export TERM=Nextfb-256

Note that in order to use this feature and italic fonts in Vim, adding
Nextfb-256 terminfo is not necessary.  Including the following lines in
the vimrc file should enable them:

  set t_ZH=[3m
  set t_Co=256
